// Worker parking: the primitive an idle OS worker thread waits on, and the
// backoff policy that governs how it waits.
//
// Stage-2 port of the idle path of runtime/sched/ (#1403). Both exist during
// the port; the scheduler port (#1364) is what consumes this.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE ZIG ACTUALLY DOES — read this before "upgrading" anything here
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The task that produced this module is titled "futex/__ulock parking shim",
// which implies the Zig scheduler parks on a futex. IT DOES NOT. `runtime/sched/`'s
// module doc says so outright:
//
//     ponytail: idle workers poll with bounded exponential backoff instead of a
//     futex/condvar wake. [...] Backoff is correct (pure polling, no missed-
//     wakeup window) and bounded; upgrade path is a futex-based wake if idle
//     latency ever matters.
//
// and `Worker.run` is exactly that:
//
//     var backoff: u64 = min_backoff_ns;
//     while (true) {
//         const t = self.findWork() orelse {
//             if (self.sched.stopping.load(.acquire)) return;
//             sleepNs(backoff);
//             backoff = @min(backoff * 2, max_backoff_ns);
//             continue;
//         };
//         backoff = min_backoff_ns;
//         ...
//
// So this module ships BOTH, with a deliberate split of authority:
//
//   - `parkSleepNs` + the backoff policy below ARE the current idle primitive.
//     They reproduce `Worker.run`'s wait exactly, so #1364 can port the run loop
//     with no behavioural change at all. This is the path the scheduler should
//     use on day one.
//   - `parkWait`/`parkWake` are the futex/`__ulock` pair the Zig names as its
//     upgrade path. They are provided because the scheduler will want them, NOT
//     because the port should switch to them silently. Switching is a scheduler
//     behaviour change (it introduces a missed-wakeup window that pure polling
//     does not have, and therefore needs an idle-worker state machine to close),
//     and that state machine is #1364's to design, not this module's.
//
// Pure polling has no missed-wakeup window because it never sleeps on a
// condition — it re-reads the world every cycle. A futex wait does sleep on a
// condition, so a wake that lands between the "no work" check and the wait is
// lost unless the waiter re-checks the word under the futex's own compare. That
// is why `parkWait` is specified as "returns when you must RE-CHECK", never as
// "returns when you were woken" — see the contract on `parkWaitReady`.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PLATFORM SEAM
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Same forced split as `runtime/alloc` (#1362) and `runtime/rand` (#1368):
// `syscall` (§11.8) is Linux-only and `extern function` (§11.7) is Darwin-only,
// and BOTH reject even an uncalled declaration, so no `hostTarget()` branch can
// carry the two paths in one file. A module is a directory (§17.1), so:
//
//   runtime/park/          this file: the backoff policy and the wait contract
//   runtime/park/linux/    futex(2) + clock_gettime/nanosleep/sched_getaffinity
//   runtime/park/darwin/   __ulock_wait/__ulock_wake + libSystem clock/sleep
//
// This file imports NEITHER provider — it holds no platform code at all — and
// the consumer binds the pair it wants.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS NOT HERE: OS THREAD CREATION
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1403 also asked for OS thread creation (`std.Thread.spawn` -> Bit). IT IS NOT
// IMPLEMENTABLE TODAY, and the missing piece is #1404, not effort:
//
//   - Darwin needs `pthread_create(&tid, nil, entry, arg)`, whose `entry` is a
//     C function pointer — a function's CODE ADDRESS.
//   - Linux is a static binary with no libc, so it needs raw `clone(2)`, whose
//     child resumes on a fresh stack and therefore cannot return into the
//     caller's frame. The child must branch to an entry address held in a
//     register. A register-indirect branch needs no relocation and IS
//     encodable in an `asm` block (§11.6) — so the Linux blocker is likewise
//     only the entry ADDRESS, not the branch.
//
// There is no source-level way to obtain one: `int(f)` on a function value is
// rejected (E0053), and `dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, ...)` on a `@symbol`-pinned Bit
// function returns null because the emitted binary carries no dynamic symbol
// table. The IR op already exists — the IR's `func_addr`, "materializes
// a function's code address as a plain pointer value [...] used to hand
// `bit_rt_spawn` its trampoline" — so #1404 is an EXPOSURE task, not a new
// capability. Both facts are recorded on #1403/#1404.
//
// Consequence for this module's testing, stated plainly because it bounds what
// the stress test can prove: a Bit program has exactly ONE OS thread that runs
// Bit code (`runtime/root/` pins the scheduler to a single worker), so `parkWake`
// cannot be observed waking a real waiter until #1404 lands. What IS proven is
// every part that can be: see tests/stress/park.

// The idle backoff window, in nanoseconds. Both values are `runtime/sched/`'s
// `min_backoff_ns`/`max_backoff_ns` unchanged (1us and 1ms). They are the whole
// of the current park policy, so they are named here rather than left as
// literals at the call site.
export const parkMinBackoffNs: int = 1000
export const parkMaxBackoffNs: int = 1000000

// `parkWait` returned and the caller MUST RE-READ the word it is waiting on.
//
// This deliberately conflates three outcomes — a real wake, a spurious wake, and
// "the word already differed from `expected`, so no wait happened" — because
// Darwin cannot tell them apart: `__ulock_wait` returns 0 both when it is woken
// and when the compare fails up front (measured, not assumed; see
// tests/stress/park). Linux CAN distinguish them (-EAGAIN vs 0), but exposing a
// distinction only one platform can honour would be a trap for the consumer, so
// the contract is the intersection of the two.
//
// That is not a loss: the correct use of any compare-and-wait primitive is a
// re-checking loop, because both platforms also permit spurious wakes. A caller
// that trusted "0 means someone woke me" would be wrong on both.
export const parkWaitReady: int = 0

// `parkWait` returned because its timeout elapsed with no wake. Distinct from
// `parkWaitReady` because it is the one outcome that carries information: it
// says the condition did NOT change within the deadline, which is what lets a
// caller bound its own wait rather than trusting the word alone.
export const parkWaitTimedOut: int = 1

// The next backoff delay after an idle cycle that found no work: double it,
// capped at `parkMaxBackoffNs`. This is `runtime/sched/`'s
// `backoff = @min(backoff * 2, max_backoff_ns)` exactly.
//
// `@nosplit` (§10.3) to match the rest of the runtime's leaf helpers: this runs
// on a worker's idle path, which must not allocate and must not reach a
// safepoint.
//
// Clamping a non-positive or over-large input to the legal window is a
// deliberate addition — the Zig cannot receive one because its `backoff` is a
// private local seeded from `min_backoff_ns`, whereas here the value is the
// CALLER's and crosses a module boundary. Without the clamp a caller that
// seeded 0 would get 0 forever, turning the idle path into an unbounded spin —
// a Power-of-10 violation (a loop whose bound is not statically provable) and
// exactly the "park that never blocks" failure this module is meant to prevent.
// PINNED (ABI.md §9): `runtime/thread` and `runtime/sched` call this across a
// module boundary, so it needs a stable symbol — an unpinned export is emitted
// as `m<N>$parkNextBackoff` with N assigned by the importing build, which no
// separately emitted object can define. `bit_rt_port_` marks it temporary, as
// in `../gc/mem.bit`; #1369 renames.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_park_next_backoff") function parkNextBackoff(cur: int): int {
  if (cur < parkMinBackoffNs) {
    return parkMinBackoffNs
  }
  let next = cur * 2
  if (next > parkMaxBackoffNs) {
    return parkMaxBackoffNs
  }
  return next
}
